steps.instructions:
Press Stop, then Run again: the level is back at 5 and the sound is on. A player expects a setting to stay. Save it once, when it changes (6.5.3). `soundChanged()` runs after every change, so at its end add:

```js
storeItem('soundLevel', level);
storeItem('soundMuted', muted);
```

`storeItem` keeps a value under a name after the sketch stops. `'soundLevel'` and `'soundMuted'` are those names: any names work, as long as step 3 reads back the same ones. Nothing reads them back yet; that is step 3.

`setup()` calls `loadSettings()` before `applySound()`, and `loadSettings()` is empty. `getItem(name)` gives back exactly what was saved under that name, a number for `level` and `true` or `false` for `muted`, or `null` if nothing was ever saved (6.5.5, 6.5.6). So use what came back only when it is not `null`:

```js
const savedLevel = getItem('soundLevel');
if (savedLevel !== null) level = savedLevel;
```

and the same for `muted`. Why not `level = getItem('soundLevel') || 5;`, the way 6.5.6 did a first run? `||` picks the 5 whenever the left side counts as false, and a saved level of `0` counts as false just like `null`: a player who turned the sound all the way down would come back to 5. `!== null` keeps the 0. Run it, change the level, mute it, press Stop and Run: the options come back as you left them.
